\section{Lower Once, Then Generate Different Artifacts}

A functional instruction set simulator (ISS) can execute one architectural transition at a time. A cycle model must also represent intermediate state, and synthesizable hardware must realize that state and its connections as circuits. VADL shares an intermediate microarchitecture synthesis pass between its cycle-accurate simulator (CAS) and hardware backends. Sections~4.7--4.9 describe how the pass resolves instruction mappings, implements decoders, assigns resource ports, lowers control logic, and makes stage connections explicit in the VADL intermediate representation (VIR) \cite{himmelbauer2024vadl}. A same-cycle connection can become a wire; a value that must survive an edge needs state. This shared lowering is distinct from the downstream synthesis that maps generated RTL to technology cells.

Listing~11 of the paper contains this actual VADL excerpt (with spacing normalized). It obtains an instruction after execute and maps its memory effects onto one stage. The surrounding ISA defines \texttt{MEM}. The excerpt does not specify a C++ call order, module interface, or RAM primitive.

\begin{verbatim}
stage MEMORY -> (ir: Instruction) = {
  let instr = EXECUTE.ir in {
    instr.write(@MEM)
    instr.read(@MEM)
    ir := instr
  }
}
\end{verbatim}

These mappings are requests to realize applicable instruction operations, not instructions to perform a write and a read for every instruction that passes through. The lowered representation must carry the conditions and data of actual reads and writes. Figure~\ref{fig:visual-334} distinguishes that shared description from the generated products. When debugging a discrepancy, identifying which side of the fork introduced it is more useful than comparing source lengths.

