\section{Represent Simultaneous Behavior in Sequential Code}

Section~4.8 describes generating C++ classes for resources and stages. Stage evaluation carries out the lowered operations; multi-step execution can keep an explicit finite-state machine and report a busy condition. Sequential host code must not accidentally publish an edge-triggered update early just because the producer's method happened to run first. In contrast, a modeled same-cycle wire must be visible before the edge to the logic that consumes it. Those requirements favor a distinction between evaluating combinational dependencies and publishing clocked updates, rather than a rule such as ``all stages run in reverse order.''

On the hardware branch, the HDL generator makes formerly global resource port references into explicit module ports and connections, emits Chisel, and lets the Chisel toolchain produce Verilog. The design can be simulated or passed to synthesis. Section~4.9 notes that arithmetic such as addition need not be assembled from a generator's library of fixed datapaths: ordinary operators can be left to synthesis. It also explains why control that cannot be eliminated can become an FSM, with busy and enable signals, rather than always fitting in one clock. A change in this control schedule may preserve ISA results while changing cycle traces and throughput. The generated HDL must still give every combinational output a value on every path and must represent an unavailable memory response as a held transaction, not as an arbitrary combinational value.

