\section{Check Edge Visibility in Both Backends}

Consider a teaching datapath with one clocked E-to-W slot, an always-ready write-back stage, and a combinational sum. The following is pseudocode for a tick; it is not a transcription of generated C++.

\begin{verbatim}
tick(execute):
    prev = ew                          # clocked state at cycle start
    candidate = add_and_tag(execute)   # combinational; invalid if idle
    write = prev if prev.valid else none
    at_edge:
        if write is not none: X[write.dest] = write.value
        ew = candidate                 # cannot feed W until next cycle
\end{verbatim}

With \texttt{ew} invalid at reset and an execute input of \(3+4\) for \(x_6\) in cycle 1, the first edge installs \((x_6,7)\) in the slot but cannot change \(x_6\). If execute is idle in cycle 2, the second edge writes \(7\) and empties the slot. With two consecutive valid inputs, W still writes precisely the previous E output at each edge. The invariant is that a valid clocked payload causes one write at its designated edge. An in-place C++ assignment before the W read could move that write one cycle earlier even though a benchmark inspecting only the final register value would still pass.

If W can refuse an input, the slot can no longer be overwritten each tick. It must hold its payload until W accepts it and either block E or buffer the new result elsewhere. In a test with two refused cycles followed by acceptance, count one write, not three. Validity signals alone do not distinguish a prepared effect from an accepted one.

Test this with an edge-aligned CAS versus Verilog trace, then compare the committed result against an ISA reference. The paper's Section~5.7 comparison with Verilator is evidence about the reported RV32IM configurations, not proof of universal cycle equivalence. Section~5.8 also reports structural measurements with Yosys on a five-stage comparison; a mapped area estimate under a demo library is not a routed chip, a clock measurement, or an assessment of power.
